.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-RESTART-MODULE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-restart-module \- restart a module's instances, one at a time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B emm restart-module
.BR \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Restarts every instance of a module without changing anything about it.
Useful for picking up a new binary after an upgrade, clearing whatever
state a long-running process has accumulated, or making a module read
its configuration file again.
.PP
Instances are restarted
.B one per reconcile tick
(roughly one every five seconds), so the rest of the pool keeps serving
while the manager works through them. A module running a single instance
is briefly unavailable while that one restarts; raise its minimum with
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-instances (1)
first if that matters.
.PP
This is the one
.B emm
request that is an event rather than a setting, and it behaves like one:
asking twice restarts the module twice, and the request is carried out
once and not repeated at the next manager start. Nothing has to be
cleared afterwards.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE
.TP
.B Desired state
Nothing about the module is recorded \- not its instance limits, not its
thread count, not whether it should be running. It is exactly the pool
it was, restarted.
.TP
.B Applications and transfer servers
are allowed here, unlike
.BR euclid-cli-emm-stop-module (1).
Because no desired state changes, there is nothing for EAP's or ETS's
own reconcile to disagree with \- the same instances come straight back.
.TP
.B A stopped module
has nothing to restart, and starting it would undo what
.B stop-module
asked for, so this is refused rather than silently ignored. Use
.BR euclid-cli-emm-start-module (1)
instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
Module to restart, e.g. \fIesm\fR. Required. Must be a module the
manager knows about \- an unknown name is rejected with
.I "HTTP 404"
rather than silently recorded.
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I name
The module that will be restarted.
.TP
.I runningInstances
How many instances the manager will cycle through, one per reconcile
tick, starting on the next one.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The restart was accepted.
.TP
.B 1
The stored session is missing or not an administrator's, the module is
unknown or stopped, the request could not be sent, or the server
rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Cycle ESM's instances after replacing its binary:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm restart-module --module esm
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch the pids change as it works through the pool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm list-modules
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make sure a single-instance module has a second instance first, so the
restart is not an outage:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-instances \e
    --module ets --min 2

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm restart-module --module ets
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-stop-module (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-start-module (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-instances (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
